<section class="pr-sec">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>How</h2>
    <p class="sub">Write once at publish. Read a file forever after.</p>
    <div class="pr-how" id="pr-how">
      <!-- publish lane -->
      <div class="h-lane">
        <div class="h-tag pub">publish · once</div>
        <div class="h-flow">
          <div class="h-node" data-k="p1">admin save</div>
          <div class="h-edge" data-k="e1"></div>
          <div class="h-node" data-k="p2">render + jinja</div>
          <div class="h-edge" data-k="e2"></div>
          <div class="h-node hot" data-k="p3">atomic write → cache/</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- serve lane -->
      <div class="h-lane">
        <div class="h-tag srv">serve · per request</div>
        <div class="h-flow">
          <div class="h-node" data-k="s1">GET /slug</div>
          <div class="h-edge" data-k="e3"></div>
          <div class="h-node" data-k="s2">hit?</div>
          <div class="h-edge" data-k="e4"></div>
          <div class="h-node hit" data-k="s3">fs::read → 200 · <b>µs–2 ms</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-wp">
          <span class="h-wp-lbl">wordpress, same request:</span>
          <div class="h-wp-bars"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><b>~600 ms</b></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- disk -->
      <div class="h-disk">
        <div class="h-cell"><span>site.db</span>content — source of truth</div>
        <div class="h-cell hot"><span>cache/</span>the served site — disposable</div>
        <div class="h-cell"><span>www/</span>uploads — back this up</div>
      </div>
      <!-- attack lane: why exploits bounce -->
      <div class="h-lane h-sec">
        <div class="h-tag atk">born unhackable — the mechanism</div>
        <div class="h-arch">
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">public request path</div>
            <div class="a-how"><code>GET /slug</code> → <code>fs::read("cache/slug/index.html")</code> → bytes out. <b>No template runs. No query executes. No interpreter exists on the public path.</b></div>
            <div class="a-kills">kills: RCE · SQLi · template injection · plugin exploits</div>
          </div>
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">/wp-admin · /xmlrpc.php</div>
            <div class="a-how">Those routes simply <b>don't exist in the router</b>. Bots get a plain 404 — same as any unknown URL.</div>
            <div class="a-kills">kills: wp-admin brute force · xmlrpc pingback/DDoS · WPSCAN fingerprinting</div>
          </div>
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">file uploads</div>
            <div class="a-how">Extension allowlist — <code>.php .phtml .phar .htaccess</code> rejected at upload. Even a bypass lands in <code>www/</code> as bytes: <b>a PHP shell is a text file here.</b></div>
            <div class="a-kills">kills: web shells · double-extension tricks · upload RCE</div>
          </div>
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">dependencies</div>
            <div class="a-how">Runtime = <b>1 static binary + 1 SQLite file</b>. No node_modules to poison, no PHP to exploit, no SaaS key to leak.</div>
            <div class="a-kills">kills: Shai-Hulud class worms · dep confusion · vendor breach</div>
          </div>
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">the admin that does exist</div>
            <div class="a-how">wp-admin-parity UI, behind: <b>argon2</b> · SQLite-backed sessions + cookie <b>HttpOnly·Secure·SameSite</b> · per-session <b>CSRF</b> · rate-limited intake.</div>
            <div class="a-kills">limits: credential stuffing · CSRF · session theft</div>
          </div>
          <div class="h-arch-row">
            <div class="a-what">cache miss path</div>
            <div class="a-how">Renders once via <b>prepared statements</b>, writes back atomically, never queries for that URL again. SQLite has no network socket to attack.</div>
            <div class="a-kills">kills: SQLi via params · DB network attacks · MySQL CVEs</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-atk-note">RPress wasn't hardened <i>after</i> the fact — <b>the architecture itself is the defense.</b> exploits need something to run <i>against</i>; the public path executes zero code.</div>
      </div>

    </div>
    <div class="pr-callout"><b>The cache is the site.</b> Idles at ~30 MB, TTFB &lt;5 ms, $5 VPS runs a fleet. nginx can front <code>cache/</code> directly — the binary is only needed for admin and misses.</div>
  </div>
</section>



